  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two proposed routes by which bacteria transfer electrons from inside the cell to an external solid surface. In both cases, electrons originate at the inner membrane and move outward across the periplasm and outer membrane toward a solid-state electron acceptor, shown above the cell envelope.\n\nOn the left, the pathway appears centered on multiheme cytochromes labeled OmcZ, Omc, OmcS, and the small periplasmic carrier PpcA. A prominent Type IV pilus passes through the outer membrane via PilQ, suggesting a conductive appendage or scaffold associated with extracellular electron transport. The red upward arrow emphasizes net electron flow out of the cell.\n\nOn the right, the model highlights the Mtr system, with CymA feeding electrons to MtrA, MtrB, and the outer-surface cytochrome MtrC, along with Omc and flavins. Here, the membrane extends into a labeled nanowire, implying electron transfer can occur along outer membrane projections decorated with cytochromes and aided by soluble flavin mediators.\n\nOverall, the image contrasts pili-associated and outer-membrane-cytochrome/nanowire-based mechanisms for microbial extracellular electron transfer.",
